=== TASK ===
# Email Verification Page

## Overview
Create a user-facing Email Verification Page that confirms a user’s email address, processes verification tokens (from links or codes), gracefully handles failures, and offers actionable next steps (retry, resend, onboarding, or dashboard access). This page should tightly integrate with the existing User Authentication system (email/password, OAuth, session management, and 2FA) and align with the Signup flow for new users who may land here as part of onboarding. The solution must be robust, accessible, and resilient to edge cases (expired tokens, invalid tokens, missing accounts), with clear user guidance and unified design language across the LifeOps SaaS platform.

## Page Description (Full Detail)
What this page is:
- A dedicated, user-facing screen that validates an email verification token (presented as a query parameter or route parameter) and updates the user’s email verification status in the backend.
- It presents three primary states: success (verification completed), failure (invalid/expired token), and pending/retry (token in transit or user needs to request a new token).

Goals:
- Verify the tokens securely, mark the user’s email as verified, initiate or resume user sessions, and enable access to onboarding or the main dashboard.
- Provide clear, accessible feedback and actions: continue onboarding, go to dashboard, resend verification email, or contact support.
- Maintain a consistent authentication context with the existing User Authentication system (email/password, OAuth, 2FA, sessions).

Connected features:
- User Authentication: Ensure verification status interacts with session management and 2FA status (e.g., if 2FA is required, prompt for 2FA setup or verification after email verification).
- Signup Page: If verification occurs during onboarding for a new user, seamlessly transition to module onboarding and first integrations setup.

UI elements and how they need to look:
- Verification Token Handler: Reads token from URL, validates on the client and server, and shows progress indicators while processing.
- Success State: Big confirmation message, a short explanation, and CTAs to continue onboarding or go to the dashboard. Optional inline microcopy about next steps (e.g., enabling 2FA).
- Failure State: Informative error message with potential causes (expired, invalid, already verified), and actions: Resend verification email, go to signup/login, or contact support.
- Retry / Resend: Button to trigger a resend flow with an input (if needed) or a simple one-click action if token was previously sent.
- Accessibility: Screen-reader friendly announcements for status changes; keyboard accessible; high-contrast text and large touch targets.
- Visual guidance: Cards with subtle shadows, rounded corners, and a primary action emphasis in blue (#4F8CFF). Status indicators use green (#5ED36D) for success, yellow (#FFD66C) for warning, and red (#EF6464) for errors, consistent with the design system.

API integrations:
- Token verification endpoint (no external APIs beyond the app’s backend): Accepts token, userId (if applicable), and returns verification result, user data (including 2FA status), and session guidance.
- Resend verification endpoint: Triggers sending a new verification email to the user’s address.
- User/session endpoints: Update user verification status, ensure session is intact or re-authenticated as needed.
- Optional: If token is tied to onboarding state, an endpoint to fetch onboarding state and next steps.

## Components to Build
- EmailVerificationPage
  - Reads token from route (e.g., /verify?token=XYZ&uid=123) and initiates verification on mount.
  - Manages local UI states: loading, success, error, and retry.
  - Triggers API calls to verify, resend, and navigate to appropriate pages (onboarding or dashboard).
  - Guards against null/undefined values when handling API results and route params.

- VerificationStatusCard
  - Presents status (success, failure, or pending) with descriptive text, accessibility-friendly ARIA roles, and action buttons.
  - Visual emphasis on primary CTA; supports quick actions like “Go to Dashboard” or “Continue Onboarding.”

- ResendVerificationButton
  - Triggers resend API; disables after click until response; shows loading/spinner and success/error feedback.

- OnboardingRedirectPanel (optional)
  - If user is in onboarding flow, shows next steps (modules, first integrations) and a CTA to proceed.

- ErrorDetailPanel
  - Shows structured error information with guidance and links to retry, contact support, or log out/login.

- Analytics/RunArtifactHook (optional)
  - Emit events for verification attempts, successes, failures for auditing and telemetry aligned with LifeOps traceability goals.

## Implementation Requirements

### Frontend
- Framework: React (with hooks) or a similar modern SPA framework used in LifeOps.
- State management: Local component state for token processing; optional global auth state via existing context/provider.
- Routing: Use the project’s routing conventions to extract token from URL params or query string.
- API handling: Use a robust fetch/axios wrapper with consistent error handling and timeouts.
- Runtime safety rules:
  - Always guard arrays: when handling any lists (e.g., possible on onboarding steps), use (data ?? []) or Array.isArray checks before map/filter.
  - Use data ?? [] for any API array results (e.g., onboarding steps, errors, or token scopes).
  - Initialize state with proper types: useState<VerificationResult | null>(null) or useState<string>("") as appropriate; for array data use useState<Type[]>([]).
  - Validate API shapes: const payload = response?.data ?? {}; const items = Array.isArray(payload?.items) ? payload.items : [];
  - Optional chaining for nested API fields: user?.emailVerified, session?.needsReauth, etc.
  - Destructure with defaults: const { status = "idle", user = {} } = response ?? {};
- UI/UX: Ensure responsive layouts with 12-column grid and card-based sections; emphasize accessibility (aria-live for status, proper contrast, focus outlines).
- Error handling: Gracefully handle token expiration, invalid tokens, and already-verified states with actionable guidance.

### Backend
- Endpoints:
  - POST /api/auth/verify-email
    - Body: { token: string, userId?: string }
    - Response: { ok: boolean, status: "verified" | "expired" | "invalid" | "already_verified", user?: { id, email, emailVerified, twoFAEnabled }, needsOnboarding?: boolean, error?: string }
  - POST /api/auth/resend-verification
    - Body: { email: string }
    - Response: { ok: boolean, sentAt?: string, error?: string }
  - GET /api/auth/session (existing)
- Database considerations:
  - Email verification tokens should be stored with expiry timestamps; tokens should be one-time use.
  - Update users.emailVerified to true only after successful verification.
  - If 2FA is configured, ensure the user is prompted accordingly after verification (e.g., route to 2FA enrollment or 2FA verification page).
- Validation:
  - Server-side validation of token presence, expiry, and association with a user.
  - Always sanitize inputs and guard against SQL injection or token leakage through error messages.

### Integration
- State flow:
  - On mount, extract token and call verify-email API.
  - If success:
    - If user needs onboarding, redirect to Onboarding flow after a short confirmation; otherwise navigate to Dashboard.
    - If 2FA setup is pending, prompt 2FA enrollment or verification.
  - If failure:
    - Show error panel with options: Resend email, retry with a new token (if user re-initiated), go to login/signup, or contact support.
- Data handling:
  - Use const tokens = (data?.tokens ?? []) or (Array.isArray(data?.tokens) ? data.tokens : []) to safely map tokens if necessary.
  - Ensure all arrays are guarded: (items ?? []).map(...), etc.
- Security:
  - Do not expose sensitive details in UI error messages.
  - Use secure cookies for session management; ensure any token in URL is single-use or strongly validated server-side.
  - Ensure all token operations require authentication context or verified tokens; avoid leaking tokens in logs.

## User Experience Flow
1. User navigates to /verify?token=ABC123 (possibly with userId) from email link.
2. Client loads and shows a loading state while verifying token with backend.
3. Backend validates token:
   - If valid and not expired:
     - Update user.emailVerified to true.
     - If onboarding is required, show a success screen with CTA “Continue Onboarding” and secondary “Go to Dashboard.”
     - If 2FA is pending, show a message to complete 2FA setup and navigate accordingly.
   - If already verified:
     - Show a friendly message: “Your email is already verified” with CTA to go to Dashboard.
   - If expired/invalid:
     - Show an error state with actionable options: “Resend verification email,” “Back to Sign In,” or “Start Onboarding” if appropriate.
4. User selects an action:
   - Resend: Trigger resend API; show confirmation.
   - Continue: Redirect to onboarding or dashboard based on user state.
   - Sign In: Redirect to login page.
5. Optional onboarding path:
   - If user is in onboarding, show quick onboarding steps (module selection, initial integrations) and a prominent CTA to proceed.

## Technical Specifications
- Data Models:
  - User: { id: string, email: string, emailVerified: boolean, twoFAEnabled?: boolean, onboardingStep?: number, needsOnboarding?: boolean }
  - EmailVerificationToken: { token: string, userId: string, expiresAt: Date, used: boolean }
- API Endpoints:
  - POST /api/auth/verify-email
  - POST /api/auth/resend-verification
  - GET /api/auth/session
- Security:
  - Verify tokens server-side with expiry and one-time-use enforcement.
  - Ensure token cannot be reused; mark as used on success.
  - Avoid exposing token payloads in client logs or error responses.
- Validation:
  - Client: sanitize token input; ensure token is non-empty string.
  - Server: validate token format, expiry, and association with a user; respond with standardized status codes and error messages.

## Acceptance Criteria
- [ ] The page loads with a token from URL and shows a loading indicator during verification.
- [ ] Successful verification updates user.emailVerified to true, and redirects or shows onboarding prompt as appropriate.
- [ ] Failed verification displays a clear, actionable error state with options to resend, retry, or sign in.
- [ ] Resend verification endpoint triggers an email send and returns a success confirmation without leaking sensitive data.
- [ ] All frontend code guards against null/undefined values when handling arrays or API responses, using data ?? [] and Array.isArray checks where applicable.
- [ ] All useState initializations for array/object data use proper defaults (e.g., useState<Something[]>([]) or useState<SomeType | null>(null)).
- [ ] The UI adheres to the design system: dark mode-first, card-based, accessible, with the specified color palette and typography guidance.

## UI/UX Guidelines
Apply the project’s design system:
- Dark mode-first, flat color blocks with depth from shadows and borders.
- 12-column grid, generous spacing (24–32px gutters), modular cards.
- Card visuals: 12–16px radii, subtle inner shadows, hover elevation.
- Navigation: Sidebar with icons, active states with bold text and accent bars; top bar with search/actions.
- Interactive elements: Primary actions in blue (#4F8CFF); error and success indicators using green #5ED36D and red #EF6464 as appropriate.
- Accessibility: High contrast text, focus outlines, aria-live regions for status announcements, and keyboard navigability.

